text:
When my cousin Leo suggested we spend the summer before our final year running a food truck, I laughed. Neither of us could cook anything more ambitious than pasta, and our combined business experience amounted to a lemonade stand we had run at the age of nine. Leo, however, had already found a second-hand van for sale and, more worryingly, had already named it.

{1}

We spent the whole of June preparing. Leo dealt with the paperwork, which turned out to be far more complicated than either of us had imagined, while I took charge of the menu. After weeks of experiments, most of them inedible, we settled on three kinds of loaded potato and a vegetarian chilli that even my sceptical grandmother pronounced 'not bad at all'.

{2}

Those first few days taught us more than any textbook could have. We learnt that people will queue for food they can see being made, that a hand-written sign attracts more customers than a printed one, and that the most important ingredient of all is speed. A customer who has waited ten minutes is rarely in the mood to be charmed.

{3}

Fortunately, help arrived from an unexpected quarter. Marisol, who had run the taco stand two pitches away for the past six summers, had been watching our struggles with what I can only describe as professional sympathy. She had, she told me later, given us about a fortnight before we sold up.

{4}

Her advice transformed the business. It also cost us our pride, since the first change meant admitting that Leo, who loved talking to customers, should be nowhere near the stove. By the middle of July we were serving twice as many people in the same amount of time, and for the first time we were making a profit, albeit a modest one.

{5}

That was the closest we came to giving up. Yet the rain also forced us to rethink our approach. Rather than waiting for customers to come to us, we started delivering to offices on the industrial estate, and within a fortnight the lunchtime orders were keeping us busy even on the wettest days.

{6}

Would I do it again? Probably not, or at least not for a while. But I learnt more about money, people and myself in those ten weeks than in two years of lectures, and whenever I walk past a food truck now, I find myself checking how long the queue is.
